      "text": "We are given a polynomial equation \\(3x^3 - 3x^2 + 11x - 8 = 0\\) with roots \\(a, b, c\\). We need to find the value of \\(ab + ac + bc\\).\n\nWe start by using Vieta's formulas, which relate the coefficients of the polynomial to sums of its roots. For the polynomial \\(3x^3 + 0x^2 - 3x + 11 = 0\\), Vieta's formulas tell us:\n\n- \\(a + b + c = -\\frac{\\text{coefficient of } x^2}{\\text{leading coefficient}} = -\\frac{0}{3} = 0\\)\n- \\(ab + ac + bc = \\frac{\\text{coefficient of } x}{\\text{leading coefficient}} = \\frac{-3}{3} = -1\\)\n- \\(abc = -\\frac{\\text{constant term}}{\\text{leading coefficient}} = -\\frac{11}{3}\\)\n\nOur goal is \\(ab + ac + bc\\).\n\nBy substituting the known sum \\(a + b + c\\) into the formula \\(ab + ac + bc\\):\n\n\\[ ab + ac + bc = ab + bc + ac = -1 \\]\n\nTherefore, the value \\(ab + ac + bc\\) is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).",
